## 7. Distribution of Source Code and Forks

You may Distribute the Work or a Fork only where all of the following conditions are satisfied.

### 7.1. Same-License Requirement
The entire Modified Work or Fork, to the extent governed by this License, must be licensed as a whole under this exact version of the Radomir Kukla Non-Commercial Source License.

You may not replace this License with another license or offer recipients the Work under alternative terms unless You have separate written authorization from every relevant rights holder.

This requirement does not override separate licenses governing identified third-party components.

### 7.2. Complete Source Requirement
You must provide the complete Corresponding Source of the version You Distribute.

The Corresponding Source must be provided:

1. together with the distributed Work;
2. through a repository or download location accessible without charge;
3. in a commonly used, editable, machine-readable format; and
4. without technological measures that prevent recipients from copying, modifying, or rebuilding the Source Code as permitted by this License.

A source-code offer that depends on payment, mandatory registration, a non-disclosure agreement, or acceptance of additional restrictions does not satisfy this requirement.

### 7.3. License Copy
You must include an unmodified copy of this License with every distributed copy of the Work.

### 7.4. Copyright and Attribution Notices
You must preserve all copyright notices, authorship notices, license notices, disclaimer notices, and Contributor attributions contained in the Work.

You may add notices identifying Your own modifications and Contributions, but You may not remove, misrepresent, or obscure existing notices except to correct a demonstrable factual error.

### 7.5. Modification Notices
You must prominently state that You modified the Work.

The notice must include:

1. a reasonable description of the modifications;
2. the date or date range of the modifications;
3. the identity or established pseudonym of the modifying Contributor;
4. the version, commit, or release from which the Modified Work was derived; and
5. a statement that the Modified Work is not an official release of the original Licensor unless the Licensor expressly authorized that representation.

A version-control history may satisfy this requirement if it remains available with the distributed Source Code and contains the required information.

### 7.6. Preservation of Source History
You must preserve the available version-control history inherited from the Original Repository up to the point of separation or synchronization, to the extent reasonably practicable.

You may not intentionally rewrite, truncate, erase, falsify, or replace inherited history for the purpose of concealing authorship, origin, copyright ownership, or earlier Contributions.

Ordinary repository maintenance, including rebasing, squashing, migration between version-control systems, removal of accidentally committed secrets, compliance with data-protection law, or correction of unlawful content, is permitted, provided that equivalent attribution and provenance records are preserved where reasonably possible.

### 7.7. Contributor Record
Every distributed Fork or Modified Work must contain a clearly visible file named:

**CONTRIBUTORS-ORIGINAL.md**

or another equivalently prominent file expressly designated by the Licensor.

That file must contain the Complete Contributor Record current as of the applicable Synchronization Date.

At minimum, it must identify:

1. the Original Repository;
2. the Synchronization Date;
3. the corresponding original commit, tag, or version, where available;
4. all Contributors credited in the Original Repository up to that point;
5. all copyright notices and attribution statements required by the Original Repository; and
6. a statement that later Contributors to the Original Repository may not be represented because the Fork diverged or ceased synchronization on the stated date.

### 7.8. Updating the Contributor Record
If You later incorporate any changes from the Original Repository, You must update the Complete Contributor Record to include Contributors credited for all incorporated changes up to the new Synchronization Date.

You are not required to continuously monitor or add Contributors whose Contributions were made to the Original Repository after the Fork permanently diverged and whose work has not been incorporated into the Fork.

You may not falsely imply that a Contributor endorses, maintains, supports, or participates in Your Fork.

### 7.9. Fork Contributor Record
You must also maintain a record of Contributors to Your Fork or Modified Work from the point of divergence onward.

The original and Fork-specific Contributor records may be kept in the same file, provided that the distinction between:

- Contributors inherited from the Original Repository; and
- Contributors who contributed after divergence

is clear and accurate.

### 7.10. No Additional Restrictions
You may not impose any additional contractual, legal, or technological restriction that prevents recipients from exercising the non-commercial rights granted under this License.

You may not require recipients to:

1. pay a license fee;
2. enter into a non-disclosure agreement;
3. accept a license that contradicts this License;
4. waive rights granted by this License; or
5. use a particular distribution platform.

Reasonable technical requirements necessary to access a hosting service, such as basic account creation, are permitted only where access to the Source Code remains free and the requirements do not materially restrict the rights granted under this License.

### 7.11. Fork Identification
A Fork must use a name or description that reasonably distinguishes it from the official Work.

You must not state or imply that the Fork is:

- an official release;
- endorsed by the Licensor;
- maintained by the Licensor; or
- affiliated with the Original Repository,

unless the Licensor has provided prior written permission.

